Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 09:00:30 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PPL's and copyrights

One idea which has been discussed for contractual enforcement of
copyright is to fingerprint each copy of a sold document.  Everyone
gets a slightly different copy.  Then if someone starts selling a bootleg
copy of the document, it may be possible to trace it to its original
source.  I've read papers which discuss how to overcome such counter
strategies as buying two copies of the document and looking for diffs
to find the places to change, then putting in random changes there
so the sold document doesn't match either of the originals.

For the case of a novel, the changes might be trivial wording changes.
For video tapes or music, perhaps we'll use a few of Tim's LSB's :-).

Hal

