Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 12:21:16 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: DIET: too much protein?

> From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
> 
> Perry Metzger:
> > ...the major effect of high protein intake is to cause your kidneys to
> > work overtime dealing with the waste products produced when your body
> > (very expensively) breaks down most of those amino acids for energy.
> 
> This may be significant, but from what I've read the biggest problem 
> is what often goes with the protein: lots of fat, often burned or
> rancid, and too much meat in the gut, which is built primarily
> like a herbivore's.

Although these are possibly problems with meat eating, I was refering to
protein intake in general. I know about people who worry that they aren't
getting enough protein as vegans and load up on all sorts of things like
tofu, even though they really have nothing to worry about, and indeed, little
reason to want to add excess protein to their diets.

> The most longevous diet, in my estimation, is low in all three of
> fats, carbohydrates, and protein in that order of importance.   

Whoa. That leaves NOTHING to eat. All sources of dietary calories come
from fats, carbohydrates and protein. (Actually, other things can yield
calories as well, like alchohol, but if you get a significant fraction of
your calorie intake from any of these things you are likely headed for
pretty bad liver failure.)  The studies I've seen seem to show
that its best if most of your calories come from complex carbohydrates,
meaning plenty of grains, vegetables, fruits, legumes, etc. You need a 
certain amount of protein (which also comes along with all of the above
quite naturally) and a certainl, likely low, amount of fat. Certainly people
do quite fine with under 10% of their calories coming from fat, and lower
still might be fine or prehaps even better.

> Especially evil are fats, glucose and sucrose.  The stuff that
> should be maximized is bulk fiber.

Its not clear that "bulk fiber" is really what you want to maximize.
Admittedly fat and refined sugars aren't great for you, but going out
and eating bran seven times a day isn't likely to do you much good, either.
The primary reason to get fiber in the diet seems to be that the digestive
system depends on it to function in a healthy way. The importance of this
shouldn't be underestimated -- low fiber and high fat seems to lead to
things like colon cancer.

Perry

